THE CLOSERS by Michael Connelly

2005. Orion. Paperback.

I'm on a Michael Connelly kick right now and this latest thriller
might be one of my favourites. It's a Cold Case (the TV show of the
same name is mentioned and might have been one of Connelly's
inspirations). Harry Bosch is back on duty after a three year
retirement and he's in the cold case department. He's given a
racially-charged case of 1988 to look at; as a small race-hate group
carry out crimes against Jews, Blacks and Mexicans.

One of the rednecks, no genius at school, steals a gun from a Jew
he's been harassing. 17 years later, DNA checks (not available in
1988) tie the redneck to a gun used in a murder made to look like a
suicide. The initial investigation was shelved as the leader of the
rednecks was himself in the LAPD; Irvin Irving killed the
investigation and now had important leverage over Internal Affairs.

There's a couple of set pieces in this book; Bosch and his offsider
(here, Kiz Rider) needing to get a phone tap authorized by an
un-cooperative judge; friction between Bosch and Irvin Irving and
some excellent misdirection of the reader's sympathies; the pesky '88
white power group are peripheral to the main crime and the real
villain is much more obvious.

There's some entertaining scenes with a tow-truck driver suspect;
Bosch tries to flush him out into a mistake and in doing so
unwittingly contributes to his murder.

I read the last 120 pages of this book at the Thai Embassy in Canberra
while committed there for the day. My mobile phone battery was low but
this book was much better entertainment than anything the phone would
have provided.

